 The local and the global view answer different questions. Cayley trees and their congruence classes describe how every forest of the family branches. Parity words and the numbers $2^l-q^k$ decide how many trees a forest has and where their cycles sit. We see six directions, in order of priority:
 \begin{enumerate}[label=(\arabic*)]
 	\item \emph{The values of the basin densities} (Problem~\ref{prob:basins}). Existence is settled in the logarithmic sense, Proposition~\ref{prop:cosets} fixes invariant-class densities (but not generally individual basin densities), and the backward mass predicts measured basin shares to about $3\,\%$, with the explicit constant $3/\log(4/3)$ (Conjecture~\ref{conj:formula}). The central open question is why: a proof that the level averages of the backward mass converge to the densities would turn this heuristic into a formula. For the mean value of the potential, the lower bound is proved (Theorem~\ref{thm:lower}), and the matching upper bound is at least as hard as the exclusion of unbounded orbits (Corollary~\ref{cor:remains}). Remark~\ref{rem:passage} expresses each density as a first-passage probability, which may be the route to such a proof and already allows numerical estimates far beyond brute-force enumeration (Table~\ref{table:passage}). Further aims are natural densities, most directly through an analogue of Mazur's natural-counting transport for $T_{3,c}$ (Remark~\ref{rem:mazur}), and the features of a cycle that determine the values. This is the direction with the largest potential for new theory, independently of the Collatz conjecture itself.
